# Item Detail Rebuild Implementation Plan

> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.

**Goal:** Turn item detail from an edit form into what the design specifies — a read-first view that shows how you actually wear a piece, with editing moved behind the `⋯` overflow.

**Architecture:** The screen inverts. `page.tsx` loads the item plus its wear history and a deterministic "goes with" set; the read view renders stats, tag rows and the complementary row; the existing form moves wholesale into a sheet opened from `⋯`. "Goes with" reuses the generator's own scoring — no new algorithm, no AI call.

**Tech Stack:** Next.js 16 App Router, Supabase, Vitest, Motion.

## Global Constraints

- **Depends on two plans**: `2026-07-24-item-data-completeness.md` (price, colours, texture) and `2026-07-24-outfit-detail-wear-logging.md` (wear data). Three of the four stat tiles are empty without them.
- **TDD, strictly.** Branch: `feat/item-detail-rebuild`. No AI attribution.
- **No AI calls on this screen.** Everything here is a query or deterministic scoring.
- Test: `npx vitest run <path>` / `npm run test:run`. Types: `npx tsc --noEmit`. Build: `npm run build`.

## Source

`design_handoff_stylist_generator/Fitcheck.dc.html:587-657`. Review: `docs/design-review-2026-07-24.md` §1.

## Decision required — read this before starting

The design's primary CTA is **"Style an outfit with this"** (`:654`). That conflicts with Decision 5
(CLAUDE.md): looks are a daily drop, generated once per local day, and tapping an occasion reads stored
rows without calling the model. Styling around a chosen item is inherently on demand.

**This plan assumes the cheap reading**, and Task 5 is written against it:

> Tapping filters **today's already-stored looks** to those containing this item and opens the first
> match. If none contain it, the button offers *"Build a look around this"*, which runs a real
> generation and **consumes the regenerate quota** (`lib/outfits/quota.ts`).

That keeps the free tier free, honours Decision 5, and gives the quota seam its first real consumer.
The alternative — an AI call on every tap — would make this button the largest per-user cost in the
product. **If you want that instead, change Task 5 before implementing it, not after.**

Second assumption: **prices are GBP**, formatted with `Intl.NumberFormat("en-GB")`, because the design
prices Pro at `£5/mo` and shows `£3.28` cost-per-wear. A currency setting belongs to the Settings plan;
until it exists, one hard-coded currency beats a fake one.

## File structure

| File | Responsibility | Change |
|---|---|---|
| `lib/closet/wear-stats.ts` | Times worn · cost/wear · last worn, from logs | **Create** |
| `lib/closet/goes-with.ts` | Deterministic complementary items | **Create** |
| `lib/closet/__tests__/` | Tests for both | **Create** |
| `components/closet/item-view.tsx` | The read view | **Create** |
| `components/closet/item-edit-sheet.tsx` | The existing form, relocated | **Create** |
| `components/closet/item-detail.tsx` | Becomes the shell that switches between them | Modify |
| `app/closet/[itemId]/page.tsx` | Loads wear history + goes-with | Modify |

