Replace everything from `function isEligible` through the end of `buildCandidates` with:

```ts
function materialExcluded(material: string | null, excludeMaterials: string[]): boolean {
  if (!material) return false;
  const m = material.toLowerCase();
  // Substring, not equality: the tagger writes free text, so "merino wool" and
  // "suede leather" are real values that an exact match silently let through.
  return excludeMaterials.some((x) => m.includes(x));
}

function isEligible(i: CandidateItem, a: CandidateArgs, excludeMaterials: string[]): boolean {
  const [lo, hi] = a.band;
  if (i.category === "Fragrance") return false; // D11: fragrances are never slotted
  if (a.excludeItemIds.includes(i.id)) return false;
  if (materialExcluded(i.material, excludeMaterials)) return false;
  // Season is deliberately NOT filtered here — see ./season.ts. It orders the
  // lists below and weights the score instead. Filtering ran against every
  // required slot, so one narrowly-tagged category zeroed the whole result.
  const f = i.formality ?? 3;
  return f >= lo - floorTolerance(i.category) && f <= hi + 0.5;
}

function bySeasonFirst(list: CandidateItem[], season: string | undefined): CandidateItem[] {
  return [...list].sort(
    (x, y) => Number(inSeason(y.seasons, season)) - Number(inSeason(x.seasons, season)),
  );
}

function isRequired(c: string): c is RequiredCategory {
  return (REQUIRED_CATEGORIES as readonly string[]).includes(c);
}

/**
 * The eligible items of every category, in-season first.
 *
 * Two soft rules live here, and `eligibility`, `missingCategory` and
 * `buildCandidates` all read from this one function so they can never disagree
 * about what the closet can do:
 *
 * 1. Season ORDERS, never excludes. The CAP in `buildCandidates` truncates the
 *    combo list, so in-season pieces must come first or a large off-season
 *    closet could push the good combos past the cap.
 * 2. Material RELIEF: a weather exclusion may narrow a REQUIRED slot but must
 *    never empty it. A closet whose only shoes are suede should get its suede
 *    shoes on a wet day, not an empty screen blaming "Shoes". Relief is scoped
 *    to weather exclusions — a formality gap is a real wardrobe gap and is still
 *    reported.
 */
export function eligibleByCategory(
  items: CandidateItem[],
  a: CandidateArgs,
): Record<string, CandidateItem[]> {
  const { excludeMaterials } = weatherRules(a.weather);
  const cats = new Set<string>(items.map((i) => i.category));
  for (const c of REQUIRED_CATEGORIES) cats.add(c);
  cats.add("Outerwear");

  const out: Record<string, CandidateItem[]> = {};
  for (const c of cats) {
    const inCat = items.filter((i) => i.category === c);
    let list = inCat.filter((i) => isEligible(i, a, excludeMaterials));
    if (!list.length && isRequired(c)) list = inCat.filter((i) => isEligible(i, a, []));
    out[c] = bySeasonFirst(list, a.season);
  }
  return out;
}

/** Per-category counts of what survived filtering — lets an empty result explain itself. */
export function eligibility(items: CandidateItem[], a: CandidateArgs): Record<string, number> {
  const by = eligibleByCategory(items, a);
  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};
  for (const c of Object.keys(by)) counts[c] = by[c].length;
  return counts;
}

/**
 * Which required slot came up empty, or null if the closet can dress this band.
 * The screen uses this to say WHICH gap blocked the outfit instead of a generic
 * "add more pieces". Outerwear is never a reason — it does not block (see below).
 */
export function missingCategory(items: CandidateItem[], a: CandidateArgs): RequiredCategory | null {
  const counts = eligibility(items, a);
  return REQUIRED_CATEGORIES.find((c) => counts[c] === 0) ?? null;
}

export function buildCandidates(items: CandidateItem[], a: CandidateArgs): CandidateItem[][] {
  const { needsOuterwear } = weatherRules(a.weather);

  const by = eligibleByCategory(items, a);
  const tops = by.Tops ?? [];
  const bottoms = by.Bottoms ?? [];
  const shoes = by.Shoes ?? [];
  const outer = by.Outerwear ?? [];
  const accessories = by.Accessories ?? [];

  const combos: CandidateItem[][] = [];
  for (const t of tops)
    for (const b of bottoms)
      for (const s of shoes) {
        // Outerwear is a PREFERENCE, not a requirement: layer it in when the cold
        // calls for it and the closet has one, but never refuse to dress someone
        // who owns no coat — that silently killed every outfit below 15°.
        // The weather strip's "Later" advice is what tells them to take a layer.
        const base = needsOuterwear && outer.length ? [t, b, s, outer[0]] : [t, b, s];
        combos.push(base); // required base (± outerwear), no accessory
        if (a.maxAccessories > 0 && accessories.length) {
          combos.push([...base, accessories[0]]); // optional, capped accent
        }
      }

  // Colour is deliberately NOT filtered here. The Refine palette is a lean, and
  // leans are expressed by ranking (`scoreCombo`'s lean term), not by exclusion —
  // the same soft-preference model as outerwear, season and materials above.
  return combos.slice(0, CAP);
}
```

Keep the imports, `CandidateItem`/`CandidateArgs` types, `CAP`, `REQUIRED_CATEGORIES` and `floorTolerance` exactly as they are above this block.

- [ ] **Step 8: Run it to verify it passes**

Run: `npx vitest run lib/generator/__tests__/candidates.test.ts`
Expected: PASS — the new relief tests plus every earlier one. In particular "eligibility reports per-category counts" must still report `Shoes: 0`, because that closet is blocked by formality, which relief does not touch.

- [ ] **Step 9: Run the whole suite**

Run: `npm run test:run`
Expected: PASS.

- [ ] **Step 10: Commit**

```bash
git add lib/generator/rules.ts lib/generator/candidates.ts lib/generator/__tests__/rules.test.ts lib/generator/__tests__/candidates.test.ts
git commit -m "feat(gen): exclude warm materials in real heat, never empty a required slot"
```

---

### Task 5: Wire the season into the generate action

**Files:**
- Modify: `app/generate/actions.ts` — imports, delete the local `currentSeason` (currently at `:32`), extend the `rankTopN` context (currently at `:145`)

**Interfaces:**
- Consumes: `currentSeason` from Task 1, the `Ctx.season` field from Task 2.
- Produces: no new exports. `candidateArgs.season` keeps its existing shape and value.

- [ ] **Step 1: Remove the local helper and import the shared one**

In `app/generate/actions.ts`, delete this block entirely:

```ts
function currentSeason(d: Date): string {
  const m = d.getMonth();
  return m < 2 || m === 11 ? "Winter" : m < 5 ? "Spring" : m < 8 ? "Summer" : "Autumn";
}
```

and add the import beside the other generator imports:

```ts
import { currentSeason } from "@/lib/generator/season";
```

Both call sites (`candidateArgs.season` and the new one in the next step) keep working unchanged — the moved function is byte-identical, and it had to move because a `"use server"` module may only export async functions, which made the old one impossible to unit-test.

- [ ] **Step 2: Pass the season into ranking**

Change the `rankTopN` call from:

```ts
    const top = rankTopN(combos, { aesthetic, band, lean: input.lean }, 20);
```

to:

```ts
    // Season rides along as a ranking preference — in-season combos float to the
    // top of the shortlist the re-ranker sees, but an off-season one is still
    // offered rather than the screen going empty.
    const top = rankTopN(combos, { aesthetic, band, lean: input.lean, season: candidateArgs.season }, 20);
```

- [ ] **Step 3: Typecheck**

Run: `npx tsc --noEmit`
Expected: no output (clean). `CandidateItem` already carries `seasons: string[]`, so it satisfies the widened `ScoreItem` with no mapping change.

- [ ] **Step 4: Run the full suite and the build**

Run: `npm run test:run && npm run build`
Expected: all tests PASS, build completes with no errors.

- [ ] **Step 5: Commit**

```bash
git add app/generate/actions.ts
git commit -m "feat(gen): rank today's looks by season fit"
```

---

### Task 6: Verify live, then open the PR

**Files:** none changed by the verification itself.

- [ ] **Step 1: Start the stack**

```bash
set -a; source .env.local; set +a; supabase start
npm run dev
```

- [ ] **Step 2: Verify on a real device viewport**

Per the global instruction, judge this in the **iOS Simulator**, not a resized desktop browser:

```bash
SIMCTL=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer/usr/bin/simctl
$SIMCTL boot "iPhone 17"   # skip if already Booted
open -a Simulator
$SIMCTL openurl booted "http://localhost:3000/generate"
$SIMCTL io booted screenshot /tmp/seasons-after.png
```

Read the screenshot AND the `npm run dev` log. Expected: three looks render as before — this is a **no-regression** check for the current month, not proof of the winter behaviour.

- [ ] **Step 3: Regenerate to confirm the ordering path runs**

Because looks are a daily drop (Decision 5), tapping an occasion reads the stored set. Press **Regenerate** once so a fresh generation actually executes with the new ranking context, then screenshot again. Confirm the dev log shows no errors and the looks still have a "why".

- [ ] **Step 4: State plainly what is and isn't verified**

The December case and the 30° heat case are covered by the deterministic tests in Tasks 1–4, not by this live run — the server derives the season from the real system clock and the temperature from Open-Meteo, and this plan adds no override for either. Say so in the PR body rather than implying winter or a heatwave was tested live.

- [ ] **Step 5: Push and open the PR**

```bash
git push -u origin fix/seasons-soft-preference
gh pr create --title "fix: season is a preference, not a filter" --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
The season tag was a hard filter applied to every required slot, so one
narrowly-tagged category could return zero outfits from a wearable closet —
trousers tagged {Spring,Summer,Autumn} meant no eligible bottoms in December and
every occasion showed the empty state.

Season now orders candidates (in-season first, so the 200-combo cap keeps the
good ones) and carries a 0.2 weight in `scoreCombo`. It can no longer eliminate
anything. Same soft-preference model as outerwear and the Refine colour lean.

The season tag was also the only thing keeping wool out of a 30° July outfit, so
this does not just delete that protection — it replaces it with the measured
equivalent. `weatherRules` now excludes warm materials above 25°, using the
temperature we already fetch: a real reading of the day rather than a label
derived from the calendar month. Material matching moved from equality to
substring, so "merino wool" and "suede leather" are actually caught.

That would have multiplied a pre-existing dead end — a closet whose only shoes
are suede returned zero outfits in the rain — so one rule now covers both: a
weather exclusion may narrow a required slot but never empty it. If it would,
the slot is refilled without the exclusion. A formality gap is still reported as
a real wardrobe gap.

Remaining trade-off: with a thin closet a mildly off-season piece can still turn
up, because ranking prefers rather than forbids ("better a slightly-off outfit
than none", as with personalBand).

Verified: full suite green, tsc clean, build clean; live no-regression check on
an iPhone 17 simulator including a Regenerate. The December and 30° behaviours
are covered by unit tests, not by the live run.
EOF
)"
```

- [ ] **Step 6: Merge once `verify` is green**

```bash
gh pr checks --watch
gh pr merge --squash --delete-branch
```

- [ ] **Step 7: Update the local state doc**

`docs/STATE.md` (gitignored, local-only) still carries the "⚠️ The same pattern remains in `seasons`" warning near the occasion-model section. Replace that paragraph with a one-line note that it was resolved on 2026-07-24 by this plan, and record the heat-material follow-up under loose ends.

