# Item Data Completeness Implementation Plan

> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.

**Goal:** Capture and expose the full truth about a garment — texture, colours, pattern, subcategory and price — from one shared vocabulary, so that every tag the AI writes is one the user can correct.

**Architecture:** One vocabulary module (`lib/closet/vocab.ts`) becomes the single source for categories, seasons, materials, textures, patterns and the colour palette; the two edit surfaces and the AI schema all read from it. A new `texture` column carries knit structure (ribbed, cable, waffle…), which `material` cannot express. The four fields the AI writes but the user could never edit — `colors`, `pattern`, `subcategory`, `price` — become editable on both surfaces.

**Tech Stack:** TypeScript 6, Zod 4, Vitest (globals, jsdom), Supabase Postgres, Anthropic structured outputs (`claude-haiku-4-5`).

## Global Constraints

- **TDD, strictly**: failing test → see it fail → minimal implementation → see it pass → commit. One commit per task.
- **Branch → PR → green CI → merge.** `main` is protected. Branch: `feat/item-data-completeness`.
- **No AI attribution** in commits, PR title or PR body.
- Test: `npx vitest run <path>` / `npm run test:run`. Types: `npx tsc --noEmit`. Build: `npm run build`.
- **Structured-output schemas must be stripped of validation keywords** — any new Zod field goes through `forStructuredOutput()` in `lib/ai/tagging-schema.ts`. `min/max/int` refinements 400 the API if sent raw. Real bounds are enforced by the Zod `.parse()` after the call.
- **Every AI tag must be user-editable** (CLAUDE.md convention). A field the model writes and the UI cannot change is a defect, not a simplification.
- Existing rows must keep working: `texture` is nullable, and no existing free-text `material` value may be destroyed by this change.

## Source of truth

`design_handoff_stylist_generator/Fitcheck.dc.html` — the twelve-screen design, verified current against the Claude Design project on 2026-07-24. Relevant sections: capture-confirm `:527-583`, item detail `:587-657`. Findings this plan implements are §2.1, §2.2, §4 and part of §1.5/§1.6 in `docs/design-review-2026-07-24.md`.

## Out of scope (deliberately — each is its own plan)

- **Texture/pattern in the generator** (scoring, weather). Cannot be tested until texture data exists; follow-up plan `item-signals-in-generator`.
- **The item-detail rebuild** (wear stats, "Goes with", "Style an outfit with this"). Needs wear logs.
- **Degendering the tagger prompt** — belongs to the women's-wardrobe work.

## File structure

| File | Responsibility | Change |
|---|---|---|
| `lib/closet/vocab.ts` | Single source for every closet vocabulary + the colour palette | **Create** |
| `lib/closet/__tests__/vocab.test.ts` | Vocab invariants (incl. "no duplicate lists elsewhere") | **Create** |
| `supabase/migrations/20260724090000_item_texture.sql` | `items.texture` column | **Create** |
| `lib/ai/tagging-schema.ts` | `texture` + constrained `material` in `TagSchema` | Modify |
| `lib/ai/tag-item.ts` | Prompt guidance for texture + the constrained material list | Modify |
| `components/capture/confirm-form.tsx` | Reads vocab; adds colour, texture, subcategory, pattern, price | Modify |
| `components/closet/item-detail.tsx` | Same fields; drops its duplicated consts; fixes the fabrication bug | Modify |
| `components/closet/color-picker.tsx` | Swatch multi-select, shared by both surfaces | **Create** |
| `lib/closet/update-schema.ts` | `UpdateSchema`, outside the Server Action module so it is testable | **Create** |
| `lib/closet/normalize-material.ts` | Maps legacy free-text materials onto the vocabulary | **Create** |
| `app/closet/[itemId]/actions.ts` | Imports `UpdateSchema`; persists the new fields | Modify |
| `app/closet/upload/actions.ts` | `confirmItem` persists the new fields | Modify |
| `scripts/backfill-item-tags.ts` | Normalises legacy free-text materials; re-tags for texture | **Create** |

---

### Task 1: The vocabulary module

**Files:**
- Create: `lib/closet/vocab.ts`, `lib/closet/__tests__/vocab.test.ts`

**Interfaces:**
- Produces: `CATEGORIES`, `SEASONS`, `FORMALITY_LABEL`, `MATERIALS`, `TEXTURES`, `PATTERNS`, `COLORS` (`{ name: string; hex: string }[]`), `colorHex(name: string): string | undefined`.

- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing test**

Create `lib/closet/__tests__/vocab.test.ts`:

```ts
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import {
  CATEGORIES, SEASONS, FORMALITY_LABEL, MATERIALS, TEXTURES, PATTERNS, COLORS, colorHex,
} from "../vocab";
import { TagSchema } from "@/lib/ai/tagging-schema";

test("categories and seasons are derived from TagSchema, so they can never drift", () => {
  expect(CATEGORIES).toEqual(TagSchema.shape.category.options);
  expect(SEASONS).toEqual(TagSchema.shape.seasons.element.options);
});

test("formality labels cover 1..5 with a blank zero slot", () => {
  expect(FORMALITY_LABEL).toHaveLength(6);
  expect(FORMALITY_LABEL[0]).toBe("");
  expect(FORMALITY_LABEL[3]).toBe("Smart casual");
});

test("textures name knit structure, which material cannot express", () => {
  expect(TEXTURES).toEqual(expect.arrayContaining(["Ribbed", "Cable knit", "Waffle", "Brushed"]));
  // A texture is never a material and vice versa — overlapping the two lists
  // would make the tagger's choice ambiguous.
  expect(TEXTURES.filter((t) => MATERIALS.includes(t))).toEqual([]);
});

test("every colour has a hex a swatch can render", () => {
  for (const c of COLORS) expect(c.hex).toMatch(/^#[0-9a-f]{6}$/i);
  expect(colorHex("navy")).toBeDefined();
  expect(colorHex("NAVY")).toBe(colorHex("navy")); // case-insensitive
  expect(colorHex("not-a-colour")).toBeUndefined();
});

test("the closet vocabulary lives ONLY in vocab.ts", () => {
  // Guards the drift that BUGS.md #14 recorded: MATERIALS/CATEGORIES/SEASONS
  // were copy-pasted into two components and neither matched the tagger.
  for (const f of [
    "components/capture/confirm-form.tsx",
    "components/closet/item-detail.tsx",
  ]) {
    const src = readFileSync(f, "utf8");
    expect(src).not.toMatch(/const\s+(MATERIALS|CATEGORIES|SEASONS|FORMALITY_LABEL)\s*[:=]/);
  }
});
```

- [ ] **Step 2: Run it to verify it fails**

Run: `npx vitest run lib/closet/__tests__/vocab.test.ts`
Expected: FAIL — `Failed to resolve import "../vocab"`.

- [ ] **Step 3: Write the implementation**

Create `lib/closet/vocab.ts`:

```ts
import { TagSchema } from "@/lib/ai/tagging-schema";

/**
 * The single source for every closet vocabulary.
 *
 * These lists were previously copy-pasted into confirm-form.tsx and
 * item-detail.tsx, and neither copy matched what the tagger was told to emit —
 * so the AI could write a material the edit screen could not offer back.
 * Categories and seasons are DERIVED from TagSchema so drift is impossible;
 * the rest are defined here and consumed by the schema.
 */

export const CATEGORIES = TagSchema.shape.category.options;
export const SEASONS = TagSchema.shape.seasons.element.options;

export const FORMALITY_LABEL = [
  "", "Very casual", "Casual", "Smart casual", "Business", "Formal",
] as const;

/** What the garment is made of. */
export const MATERIALS = [
  "Cotton", "Wool", "Merino wool", "Cashmere", "Linen", "Silk", "Denim",
  "Leather", "Suede", "Canvas", "Corduroy", "Tweed", "Fleece", "Down",
  "Polyester", "Nylon", "Viscose", "Lyocell", "Stainless steel", "Gold",
  "Silver", "Rubber", "Other",
] as const;

/**
 * How the fabric is built — knit structure and surface finish. This is a
 * SEPARATE dimension from material: a ribbed merino knit and a flat merino
 * shirt are the same material and read completely differently, both in warmth
 * and in formality. There was no field for it, so that information was lost.
 */
export const TEXTURES = [
  "Flat", "Ribbed", "Cable knit", "Waffle", "Chunky knit", "Fine knit",
  "Brushed", "Fleece-back", "Twill", "Herringbone", "Quilted", "Pile",
] as const;

export const PATTERNS = ["solid", "striped", "check", "print", "other"] as const;

/**
 * The palette the swatch picker offers. Names match what the tagger emits and
 * what `lib/generator/color.ts` groups into families — a colour the generator
 * cannot place is a colour that silently scores nothing.
 */
export const COLORS = [
  { name: "black", hex: "#141414" },
  { name: "charcoal", hex: "#36353a" },
  { name: "grey", hex: "#8a8a8f" },
  { name: "white", hex: "#f4f1ea" },
  { name: "cream", hex: "#ece3d2" },
  { name: "stone", hex: "#cabfae" },
  { name: "beige", hex: "#d5c4a8" },
  { name: "camel", hex: "#b08d57" },
  { name: "tan", hex: "#a9784f" },
  { name: "brown", hex: "#6b4b32" },
  { name: "rust", hex: "#b86a47" },
  { name: "burgundy", hex: "#5e2733" },
  { name: "olive", hex: "#5e7256" },
  { name: "green", hex: "#3f6b4a" },
  { name: "navy", hex: "#242f45" },
  { name: "blue", hex: "#3d5a80" },
  { name: "denim", hex: "#5a7896" },
  { name: "pink", hex: "#d0a0a0" },
  { name: "yellow", hex: "#d6b756" },
  { name: "silver", hex: "#c0c0c8" },
  { name: "gold", hex: "#c9a227" },
] as const;

export function colorHex(name: string): string | undefined {
  const n = name.toLowerCase();
  return COLORS.find((c) => c.name === n)?.hex;
}
```

- [ ] **Step 4: Run it to verify it passes**

Run: `npx vitest run lib/closet/__tests__/vocab.test.ts`
Expected: the first four tests PASS. **"the closet vocabulary lives ONLY in vocab.ts" still FAILS** — the components are not migrated until Task 4. Leave it red and say so in the commit; it turns green in Task 4.

If the runner's cwd makes the `readFileSync` paths fail, use `new URL("../../../components/...", import.meta.url)` instead of a bare relative path.

